Cell["\<\
Inside Object, we have the fields of the type that we specified in the first \
cell (bulk, constrained, boundary or gauge).  The entries of Obj correspond \
to [[time step, x component, y component ,u component]].
Keep in mind that Jecco uses subdomain for the u domain. Each of the field \
will be characterized by a different key (this is how the hdf5 module of \
Mathematica works). This key will differ not only for each different \
function, but also for each different subdomain. So, the same function, will \
have two different keys in different sub domains (I am repeating myself on \
purpuse  here, just to be really clear.)
Let\[CloseCurlyQuote]s take a look at the different keys:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.902832481709796*^9, 3.902832520038484*^9}, {
  3.924594894111813*^9, 3.92459504679552*^9}, {3.924595091695109*^9, 
  3.924595243591055*^9}, {3.940415765716448*^9, 
  3.940415793814733*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6aebf12d-6feb-4aed-a499-\
10e7265a3d85"],

